        "id": "ch5_4-1",
        "text": "Executing TDD\nTDD is a well-known practice in software engineering. This is a software development\napproach in which test cases are written first before writing any code for a required feature\nin an application. Here are the three simple rules of TDD:\n\u2022I Do not write any functional code unless you write a unit test that is failing.\n\u2022I Do not write any additional code in the same test more than you need to make the\ntest fail.\n\u2022I Do not write any functional code more than what is needed to pass a failing test.\nThese TDD rules also drive us to follow a famous three-phase approach of software\ndevelopment called Red, Green, Refactor . The phases are repeated continuously for TDD.\nThese three phases are shown in Figure 5.4 and are described next.\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Red\n\u2022 Green\n\u2022 Refactor\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
